ebounds as Boston edged Philadelphia 125–120 in overtime, according to the AP and the official box score.","## Helicopter commute","Asked about his reported helicopter commutes to games, James laughed it off: \"I've done it before … just another way of transportation.\" The quip fit the evening's mood — a spectacle-first finale with the regular season still to come.","The moment that mattered was the slam: a reminder that even in year 24, James can still stop a building. His regular-season debut in a Sixers uniform comes Oct. 20 at Madison Square Garden against the Knicks — with the whole league watching to see what the encore looks like."],"category":"Sports","tags":["LeBron James","Philadelphia 76ers","NBA","Boston Celtics","Tyrese Maxey"],"author":"NewsVeria Newsroom","publishedAt":"2026-10-17T08:19:49Z","image":{"src":"/images/media-generation-nv-lebron-sixers-debut-slam-2026-10-17.webp","alt":"Editorial illustration of a basketball player silhouette mid-dunk over a hoop before a dotted arena crowd in navy and light blue","caption":"Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.","credit":"NewsVeria generated illustration"},"sources":[{"name":"Associated Press (Dan Gelston) — LeBron James calls helicopter rides transportation after he soars on big dunk in 76ers home","url":"https://www.ottumwacourier.com/sports/national_sports/lebron-james-calls-helicopter-rides-transportation-after-he-soars-on-big-dunk-in-76ers-home/article_2f9a3f79-e79d-59ec-a092-5b97688e7938.html","role":"primary"},{"name":"ESPN — LeBron fires up crowd with monster slam in home debut","url":"https://players.bio/news/lebron-fires-up-crowd-with-monster-slam-in/907911","role":"corroboration"}],"status":"PUBLISHED","verification":{"eventConfirmed":true,"primarySourceFound":true,"independentCorroboration":true,"chronologyChecked":true,"detailsChecked":true,"supersededChecked":true,"imageChecked":true,"reviewer":"NewsVeria editorial desk (AI-assisted, standards in Editorial Policy)","notes":"AP wire (Gelston, Philadelphia) opened and read in full; Sportradar box score confirms Celtics 125-120 OT at Xfinity Mobile Arena, Oct 16. Attendance 19,746, alley-oop from Maxey, chalk toss, helicopter quote all in the AP text. No newer development."},"editorPick":true,"breaking":false,"pinned":false,"pinUrl":null},{"id":"art-0158-alcs-game4-postponed-2026-10-17","slug":"alcs-game-4-postponed-rain-rays-guardians","headline":"Rain Pushes Rays–Guardians ALCS Game 4 to Saturday as Sunday Becomes a Game Day","socialHeadline":"Cleveland's rain wiped out Friday's Game 4 — now the series plays Saturday AND Sunday, and the scheduled off-day is gone","summary":"Friday night's ALCS Game 4 between the Tampa Bay Rays and Cleveland Guardians at Progressive Field was postponed by day-long rain, pushing Game 4 to Saturday at 8 p.m. ET and turning Sunday's scheduled off-day into Game 5. Cleveland leads the series 2–1.","body":["Day-long rain in Cleveland has rewritten the American League Championship Series schedule: Friday night's Game 4 between the Tampa Bay Rays and the Guardians was postponed about an hour after its 8:08 p.m. ET scheduled first pitch at Progressive Field, the league announced.","Game 4 moves to Saturday at 8 p.m. ET, and Sunday's planned off-day has been erased — Game 5 is now set for Sunday at 5:08 p.m. ET. If the series extends, Game 6 would be Monday, Oct. 19 and Game 7 Tuesday, Oct. 20 at Tampa Bay. The Guardians lead the best-of-seven 2–1.","## Pitching unchanged","The delay does not shuffle the rotations: Cleveland will still open with Shawn Armstrong in a bullpen game, and Tampa Bay stays with Nick Martinez, per reporting on the league's announcement.","The winner advances to a World Series that already has half its matchup set — the Milwaukee Brewers swept the Los Angeles Dodgers in the NLCS, and the Fall Classic opens Oct. 23."],"category":"Sports","tags":["MLB","ALCS","Cleveland Guardians","Tampa Bay Rays","postponed"],"author":"NewsVeria Newsroom","publishedAt":"2026-10-17T08:19:49Z","image":{"src":"/images/media-generation-nv-alcs-game4-rain-postponed-2026-10-17.webp","alt":"Editorial illustration of a baseball stadium under rain clouds with a calendar showing a rescheduled date in navy and light blue","caption":"Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.","credit":"NewsVeria generated illustration"},"sources":[{"name":"USA Today (AP/MLB announcement) — Guardians-Rays ALCS Game 4 postponed by rain","url":"https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/mlb/playoffs/2026/10/16/guardians-rays-score-highlights-alcs-game-4--live/92271174007/","role":"primary"},{"name":"NBC Sports (AP) — ALCS Game 4 postponed by rain; Rays and Guardians to resume series Saturday","url":"https://www.nbcsports.com/mlb/news/alcs-game-4-postponed-by-rain-rays-and-guardians-to-resume-series-saturday","role":"corroboration"}],"status":"PUBLISHED","verification":{"eventConfirmed":true,"primarySourceFound":true,"independentCorroboration":true,"chronologyChecked":true,"detailsChecked":true,"supersededChecked":true,"imageChecked":true,"reviewer":"NewsVeria editorial desk (AI-assisted, standards in Editorial Policy)","notes":"MLB announcement confirmed via AP/USA Today live blog and NBC Sports; dates/times (Sat 8 p.m. ET Game 4, Sun 5:08 p.m. ET Game 5, Mon/Tue at TB if needed) consistent. Series state 2-1 Guardians, starters Armstrong/Martinez confirmed. Game 4 outcome not yet known — story covers the scheduling only."},"editorPick":false,"breaking":true,"pinned":false,"pinUrl":null},{"id":"art-0159-thwaites-glacier-melting-2026-10-17","slug":"thwaites-doomsday-glacier-melting-faster-study","headline":"'Doomsday Glacier' Melting Far Faster Than Thought, New Study Finds","socialHeadline":"Thwaites Glacier could raise seas ~1.2 inches by 2100 — twice what models expected — even with no further warming","summary":"A peer-reviewed study in Nature Communications finds that Antarctica's Thwaites Glacier could contribute about 1.2 inches of sea-level rise by 2100 — twice the average of earlier model ensembles — because warm seawater is flushing through a kilometers-wide \"grounding zone\" beneath the ice. There is roughly a 60% chance the glacier detaches from its stabilizing ridge by 2100.","body":["Antarctica's Thwaites Glacier — the \"Doomsday Glacier\" holding about 65 centimeters of potential sea-level rise — is melting far more aggressively than previous models assumed, according to a peer-reviewed study published Oct. 12 in Nature Communications.","The UC Irvine and NASA JPL team, led by Mattia Poinelli with Eric Rignot, calibrated their ice-sheet model against 27 years of satellite data instead of assuming a fixed \"grounding line\" where the glacier meets the sea. The satellite record shows a kilometers-wide \"grounding zone\": warm seawater flushes in and out with the tides — \"tidal pumping\" — melting the ice from below.","## Twice the expected sea-level contribution","The result: Thwaites could add 30.1 ± 5.7 millimeters — about 1.2 inches — of sea-level rise by 2100, twice the mean of the ISMIP6 model ensemble, even without additional warming. Simulations using a grounding zone were four times more likely to reproduce the glacier's observed 25-year retreat: roughly 25 kilometers since 1996, about 800 meters a year on the main trunk.","The study puts the probability that Thwaites \"unpins\" from the Mouginot Ridge — the seafloor high currently holding it back — at around 60% by 2100, and as early as 2038 where grounding zones stretch 5 to 6 kilometers. Past that point, retreat would accelerate across downward-sloping bedrock toward the next ridge.","The authors stress this is one glacier's revised forecast, not a global total — but for coastal planners, the direction of the revision is the message: the models have been running cool."],"category":"Science","tags":["Thwaites Glacier","Antarctica","sea level rise","climate","Nature Communications"],"author":"NewsVeria Newsroom","publishedAt":"2026-10-17T08:19:49Z","image":{"src":"/images/media-generation-nv-thwaites-glacier-study-2026-10-17.webp","alt":"Editorial illustration of a glacier cross-section with warm water flowing beneath it and a rising sea-level gauge in navy and light blue","caption":"Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.","credit":"NewsVeria generated illustration"},"sources":[{"name":"Nature Communications — Poinelli et al.: Sea-level contribution from Thwaites Glacier doubles in an observation-constrained ice-sheet model","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-77976-1","role":"primary"},{"name":"CNN — Scientists knew the Doomsday Glacier was melting. They didn't realize how 'aggressively'","url":"https://www.cnn.com/2026/10/15/climate/thwaites-melt-study-doomsday-glacier?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta","role":"corroboration"}],"status":"PUBLISHED","verification":{"eventConfirmed":true,"primarySourceFound":true,"independentCorroboration":true,"chronologyChecked":true,"detailsChecked":true,"supersededChecked":true,"imageChecked":true,"reviewer":"NewsVeria editorial desk (AI-assisted, standards in Editorial Policy)","notes":"Peer-reviewed paper opened and read in full; DOI 10.1038/s41467-026-77976-1. Key numbers (30.1 ± 5.7 mm, 2x ISMIP6 mean, 60% unpinning probability, 2038 earliest) taken from the paper. CNN + phys.org independently consistent. A separate Oct 13 British Antarctic Survey Thwaites study is consistent, not superseding."},"editorPick":true,"breaking":false,"pinned":false,"pinUrl":null},{"id":"art-0160-kim-jones-hong-kong-flagship-2026-10-17","slug":"kim-jones-own-label-hong-kong-flagship","headline":"Kim Jones Revives His Own Label With a Hong Kong Flagship Opening in 2027","socialHeadline":"After seven years at Dior Men, Kim Jones is going back under his own name — first global flagship lands in Hong Kong next October","summary":"Former Dior Men artistic director Kim Jones is reviving his eponymous label, announcing a first global flagship at Hong Kong's Central Yards — a 4,000 sq ft duplex opening October 2027 — with a debut FW27 collection and an online launch in mid-2027.","body":["Kim Jones is going back under his own name. The former Dior Men artistic director announced this week that his revived eponymous label, \"KIM JONES,\" will open its first global flagship at Central Yards on Hong Kong's New Central Harbourfront — a two-storey, roughly 4,000-square-foot duplex developed by Henderson Land, opening in October 2027.","\"After many years working with incredible houses and collaborators, it feels like the right moment to do something under my own name again,\" Jones said in the announcement, which Henderson Land confirmed on Oct. 13.","## The plan","The debut FW27 collection is expected first, followed by an online launch at kimjones.com in mid-2027, then the flagship. Production will be mostly made in Japan, with leather goods from Italy, and the pricing is pitched as \"considered\" — a top coat reportedly around €1,300. Jones is co-running the label with Lucy Beeden, and the pair are its majority owners.","Jones left Dior Men in January 2025 after seven years, having departed Fendi in 2024. His original namesake label ran from 2003 to 2008 — so this is less a launch than a return, with Hong Kong chosen as its first home.","Henderson Land chairman Martin Lee Ka-shing framed the deal as a signal for the district: a global luxury debut choosing the harborfront for its first door anywhere in the world."],"category":"Business","tags":["Kim Jones","fashion","Hong Kong","flagship","luxury retail"],"author":"NewsVeria Newsroom","publishedAt":"2026-10-17T08:19:49Z","image":{"src":"/images/media-generation-nv-kim-jones-hk-flagship-2026-10-17.webp","alt":"Editorial illustration of a luxury flagship storefront with a mannequin, shopping bag and hanger icons in navy and light blue","caption":"Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.","credit":"NewsVeria generated illustration"},"sources":[{"name":"DimSum Daily (quoting Henderson Land) — Kim Jones to open first global flagship at Central Yards in Hong Kong in 2027","url":"https://www.dimsumdaily.hk/kim-jones-to-open-first-global-flagship-at-central-yards-in-hong-kong-in-2027/","role":"primary"},{"name":"Hypebeast — Kim Jones announces eponymous label with FW27 debut collection, Hong Kong store opening","url":"https://hypebeast.com/2026/10/kim-jones-announces-eponymous-label-with-fw27-debut-collection-hong-kong-store-opening-news","role":"corroboration"}],"status":"PUBLISHED","verification":{"eventConfirmed":true,"primarySourceFound":true,"independentCorroboration":true,"chronologyChecked":true,"detailsChecked":true,"supersededChecked":true,"imageChecked":true,"reviewer":"NewsVeria editorial desk (AI-assisted, standards in Editorial Policy)","notes":"Henderson Land announcement (Oct 13) quoted across independent outlets (Hypebeast, Esquire SG, The Peak MY, DimSum Daily) with consistent facts: Central Yards, ~4,000 sq ft duplex, Oct 2027, FW27 debut, mid-2027 online launch, Beeden co-ownership, made-in-Japan/Italy production. No newer development."},"editorPick":false,"breaking":false,"pinned":false,"pinUrl":null},{"id":"art-0161-dt109-fatty-liver-2026-10-17","slug":"dt109-drug-reverses-fatty-liver-gut-repair-2026","headline":"Experimental Drug Reverses Severe Fatty Liver Disease in Animal Studies by Repairing the Gut","socialHeadline":"An experimental compound called DT-109 reversed severe fatty liver disease in mice and monkeys by fixing the gut — but it has not been tested in people yet","summary":"A glycine-based tripeptide called DT-109 reversed a severe form of fatty liver disease in mice and nonhuman primates by repairing the gut barrier and cutting the flow of toxins to the liver, researchers at Michigan Medicine reported. The work is strictly preclinical — no human trials have been announced — and any future drug is years away.","body":["An experimental compound has reversed severe fatty liver disease in mice and monkeys by targeting the gut rather than the liver itself, researchers at Michigan Medicine reported in a peer-reviewed study. The findings, published in The Journal of Clinical Investigation, point to the gut-liver axis as a promising therapeutic target for a disease that currently has few treatment options.","The compound, a glycine-based tripeptide called DT-109, was tested against metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH) — the aggressive form of fatty liver disease that can progress to cirrhosis and liver failure. In the animal studies, DT-109 reduced levels of the harmful gut bacterium Clostridium perfringens and its ammonia byproducts, restored the gut's barrier function, and blocked the flow of bacterial toxins from the intestine to the liver, according to the university's announcement.","## A gut-first approach","Lead researcher Eugene Chen's team showed that the treatment not only halted the disease but reversed it in the treated animals. Rather than suppressing liver inflammation directly, the drug candidate works upstream: by repairing the leaky gut barrier that lets toxins pour into the bloodstream and reach the liver. MedicalXpress, reporting independently on the study, noted the work as a notable preclinical advance.","## Years from any pharmacy shelf","The university has patented the compound and licensed it to Diapin Therapeutics, but no human clinical trials had been announced as of October 2026. Experts not involved in the work caution that most animal-study results do not survive the jump to humans, so the findings should be seen as early scientific progress — not an available or imminent treatment. Patients with fatty liver disease should not change any treatment based on this research."],"category":"Health","tags":["Fatty liver disease","MASH","DT-109","gut microbiome","Michigan Medicine"],"author":"NewsVeria Newsroom","publishedAt":"2026-10-18T01:49:07Z","image":{"src":"/images/media-generation-nv-dt109-fatty-liver-2026-10-17.webp","alt":"Editorial illustration of a stylized liver connected to a glowing gut pathway with molecule dots and repair arrows in navy and light blue","caption":"Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.","credit":"NewsVeria generated illustration"},"sources":[{"name":"ScienceDaily — Michigan Medicine press release on the DT-109 study","url":"https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/07/260711010116.htm","role":"primary"},{"name":"MedicalXpress (Noah Fromson) — independent reporting on the drug candidate","url":"https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-07-drug-candidate-severe-fatty-liver.html","role":"corroboration"}],"status":"PUBLISHED","verification":{"eventConfirmed":true,"primarySourceFound":true,"independentCorroboration":true,"chronologyChecked":true,"detailsChecked":true,"supersededChecked":true,"imageChecked":true,"reviewer":"NewsVeria editorial desk (AI-assisted, standards in Editorial Policy)","notes":"Michigan Medicine release opened and read; DT-109 reversed MASH in mice and nonhuman primates via reduced Clostridium perfringens/ammonia, restored gut barrier, blocked toxin flow (JCI, DOI 10.1172/JCI200522). MedicalXpress independently consistent. Strictly preclinical — no human trials announced; U-M patented, licensed to Diapin Therapeutics. Article explicitly framed as animal studies."},"editorPick":false,"breaking":false,"pinned":false,"pinUrl":null},{"id":"art-0162-roman-telescope-focus-2026-10-17","slug":"roman-space-telescope-focus-beats-expectations-2026","headline":"NASA's Roman Space Telescope Sharpens Focus to Nearly 30% Better Than Predicted","socialHeadline":"During commissioning, NASA's Roman telescope hit 63 nanometers of optical error against a 90-nanometer requirement — nearly 30% sharper than expected","summary":"During in-space commissioning, the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope's main instrument measured nearly 30% sharper than its design requirement after a secondary-mirror adjustment of about one-sixteenth the width of a human hair, NASA reported. First public images are expected in early 2027.","body":["NASA's newest flagship observatory is seeing sharper than expected. During in-space commissioning of the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope, engineers measured the Wide Field Instrument's optical error at 63 nanometers RMS — against a requirement of 90 nanometers — putting the telescope's focus nearly 30% better than predicted, the agency announced on its mission blog.","The milestone came after a delicate adjustment to the telescope's secondary mirror of roughly one-sixteenth the diameter of a human hair, according to mission engineer Ashley Balzer's account of the commissioning work. The team also corrected a slight defocus in the Coronagraph instrument, Roman's technology-demonstration tool for imaging planets directly around other stars.","## What Roman is for","Roman, which launched on August 30, 2026, is designed to survey vast swaths of the infrared sky to study dark energy, map the structure of the universe and census exoplanets. Its wide field of view — about 100 times that of Hubble — paired with Hubble-class resolution is what makes the optical performance so important: sharper images over a wider field mean cleaner data for cosmology.","Independent space coverage from Sprouts Space, citing the NASA update, reported the same figures and noted the mission remains in commissioning, with the first public science images expected in early 2027.","## Still in checkout","The telescope is not yet in full science operations; the commissioning phase is expected to continue into the new year. NASA said the sharper-than-required optics give the mission margin that could translate into more precise measurements once the survey begins."],"category":"Science","tags":["NASA","Roman Space Telescope","astronomy","Wide Field Instrument","space"],"author":"NewsVeria Newsroom","publishedAt":"2026-10-18T01:49:07Z","image":{"src":"/images/media-generation-nv-roman-telescope-2026-10-17.webp","alt":"Editorial illustration of a space telescope with an expanding ring of light over a starfield and an upward trend chart in navy and light blue","caption":"Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.","credit":"NewsVeria generated illustration"},"sources":[{"name":"NASA Science — Roman mission blog, Oct 14, 2026 (Ashley Balzer)","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/roman/2026/10/14/nasa-roman-team-checks-out-main-instrument-sharpens-telescopes-focus/","role":"primary"},{"name":"Sprouts Space, Oct 14, 2026 — independent coverage citing the NASA update","url":"https://sproutsspace.com/blogs/news/roman-telescope-sharpens-focus-wide-field-instrument","role":"corroboration"}],"status":"PUBLISHED","verification":{"eventConfirmed":true,"primarySourceFound":true,"independentCorroboration":true,"chronologyChecked":true,"detailsChecked":true,"supersededChecked":true,"imageChecked":true,"reviewer":"NewsVeria editorial desk (AI-assisted, standards in Editorial Policy)","notes":"NASA Roman mission blog (Oct 14, 2026) opened and read: WFI optics 63 nm RMS vs 90 nm requirement (~30% better), secondary-mirror adjustment ~1/16 human-hair diameter, Coronagraph defocus corrected. Launch Aug 30, 2026 confirmed. Sprouts Space independently consistent. Still in commissioning; first public images early 2027. No superseding development."},"editorPick":true,"breaking":false,"pinned":false,"pinUrl":null},{"id":"art-0163-spacex-dragon-crs35-2026-10-17","slug":"spacex-dragon-crs35-strange-cargo-iss-2026","headline":"SpaceX Dragon Delivers Final Solar Arrays and Odd Science Cargo — Including Lab-Grown Retinas — to the ISS","socialHeadline":"SpaceX's CRS-35 Dragon docked with the space station carrying 6,300+ lbs of cargo — from the final rollout solar arrays to artificial retinas and brain organoids","summary":"SpaceX's Dragon cargo capsule docked with the International Space Station on Oct. 15 after launching from Cape Canaveral two days earlier with more than 6,300 pounds of supplies and science gear. Alongside the final pair of roll-out solar arrays, the manifest included some unusual payloads: artificial retinas to be manufactured in microgravity, 3D heart-tissue models, a glass-formation study and brain organoids for neurodegenerative-disease research.","body":["A SpaceX Dragon capsule arrived at the International Space Station on the morning of October 15, 2026, completing the company's 35th contracted resupply flight for NASA. The Falcon 9 rocket lifted off from Cape Canaveral at 6:33 a.m. EDT on October 13, and Dragon docked autonomously at 10:27 a.m. EDT two days later to the space-facing port of the Harmony module, NASA confirmed.","The flight carried more than 6,300 pounds of cargo, including the final pair of the station's roll-out solar arrays — the last pieces of a multi-year power upgrade for the aging orbital laboratory.","## The strange cargo","Tucked in with the usual supplies were some decidedly unusual science payloads. Among them: hardware for manufacturing artificial retinas in microgravity, 3D models of heart tissue, a new study of how glass forms without gravity's interference, and brain organoids destined for research into neurodegenerative diseases. NASASpaceFlight's independent launch coverage reported the same manifest and timeline.","## Why microgravity matters for this cargo","Several of the payloads exploit the station's weightless environment to do things that are difficult or impossible on Earth. Growing delicate tissues and forming flawless materials without gravity pulling on them can yield insights for medicine and manufacturing — a key reason the ISS continues to host such a varied research portfolio in its later years.","The docking went smoothly and the crew has begun unpacking, NASA said. Dragon will remain berthed at the station for several weeks before returning to Earth with research samples and completed experiments."],"category":"Science","tags":["SpaceX","NASA","International Space Station","Dragon","CRS-35"],"author":"NewsVeria Newsroom","publishedAt":"2026-10-18T01:49:07Z","image":{"src":"/images/media-generation-nv-spacex-dragon-cargo-2026-10-17.webp","alt":"Editorial illustration of a stylized cargo capsule docking with a space station, surrounded by science icons in navy and light blue","caption":"Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.","credit":"NewsVeria generated illustration"},"sources":[{"name":"NASA news release — science and cargo launch on the 35th SpaceX resupply mission","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-science-cargo-launch-on-35th-spacex-resupply-mission-to-station/","role":"primary"},{"name":"NASASpaceFlight — independent CRS-35 launch coverage","url":"https://www.nasaspaceflight.com/2026/10/crs-35-launch/","role":"corroboration"}],"status":"PUBLISHED","verification":{"eventConfirmed":true,"primarySourceFound":true,"independentCorroboration":true,"chronologyChecked":true,"detailsChecked":true,"supersededChecked":true,"imageChecked":true,"reviewer":"NewsVeria editorial desk (AI-assisted, standards in Editorial Policy)","notes":"NASA CRS-35 release opened and read: Falcon 9/Dragon launched 6:33 a.m. EDT Oct 13 from Cape Canaveral, 6,300+ lbs cargo incl. final ISS roll-out solar array pair; docked 10:27 a.m. EDT Oct 15 to Harmony's space-facing port. Unusual cargo confirmed: artificial-retina manufacturing hardware, 3D heart-tissue models, glass-formation study, brain organoids. NASASpaceFlight independently consistent. No superseding development."},"editorPick":false,"breaking":false,"pinned":false,"pinUrl":null},{"id":"art-0164-valkyries-dream-game1-2026-10-17","slug":"valkyries-dream-game-1-wnba-finals-williams-2026","headline":"Valkyries Edge Dream 68-64 in Game 1 of WNBA Finals Behind Williams' 26","socialHeadline":"Gabby Williams dropped 26 and hit the go-ahead and clinching free throws as the Valkyries took Game 1 of the WNBA Finals 68-64 over Atlanta","summary":"The Golden State Valkyries beat the Atlanta Dream 68-64 in Game 1 of the WNBA Finals on Saturday night at Chase Center in San Francisco. Gabby Williams scored a game-high 26 points and sealed it at the line in the final 1:18, completing a comeback from a 64-61 deficit. Game 2 is Monday in San Francisco.","body":["The Golden State Valkyries are one win into their first WNBA championship series. The second-year franchise beat the Atlanta Dream 68-64 on Saturday night in Game 1 of the WNBA Finals at Chase Center in San Francisco, in a defensive battle that came down to the final minute.","Gabby Williams delivered the performance of the night: a game-high 26 points, including the go-ahead and clinching free throws in the final 1:18. Golden State closed the game on a run after trailing 64-61, and Atlanta did not score over the final three-plus minutes.","## A defensive battle","NBC Sports' game report described a heavily anticipated defensive struggle, with both teams grinding through long scoreless stretches. Sky Sports' independent account of the same game confirmed the 68-64 final, Williams' 26 points and the decisive late free throws.","## What comes next","Game 2 is scheduled for Monday, October 19, back in San Francisco, before the series shifts venues. The Valkyries, who swept the Aces in the semifinals, are chasing the franchise's first title in just their second season of existence."],"category":"Sports","tags":["WNBA","Golden State Valkyries","Atlanta Dream","Gabby Williams","WNBA Finals"],"author":"NewsVeria Newsroom","publishedAt":"2026-10-18T01:49:07Z","image":{"src":"/images/media-generation-nv-valkyries-game1-2026-10-17.webp","alt":"Editorial illustration of a basketball hoop with a ball mid-flight and a 'series 1-0' scoreboard motif in navy and light blue","caption":"Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.","credit":"NewsVeria generated illustration"},"sources":[{"name":"NBC Sports — game report, Valkyries take Game 1 of WNBA Finals over Dream","url":"https://www.nbcsports.com/wnba/news/golden-state-valkyries-take-game-1-of-wnba-finals-over-atlanta-dream-in-heavily-anticipated-defensive-battle","role":"primary"},{"name":"Sky Sports — independent secondary reporting on Game 1","url":"https://www.skysports.com/nba/news/13600326/wnba-finals-gabby-williams-stars-as-golden-state-valkyries-beat-atlanta-dream-in-wnba-finals-game-1","role":"corroboration"}],"status":"PUBLISHED","verification":{"eventConfirmed":true,"primarySourceFound":true,"independentCorroboration":true,"chronologyChecked":true,"detailsChecked":true,"supersededChecked":true,"imageChecked":true,"reviewer":"NewsVeria editorial desk (AI-assisted, standards in Editorial Policy)","notes":"NBC Sports game report opened and read: Valkyries 68-64 over Dream, Game 1 WNBA Finals, Sat Oct 17 2026, Chase Center; Williams 26 pts incl. go-ahead/clinching FTs in final 1:18; comeback from 64-61; Atlanta scoreless final 3+ min. Sky Sports independently consistent. Game 2 Mon Oct 19. Not a duplicate: existing slug covers semifinal sweep of Aces, not the Finals."},"editorPick":true,"breaking":true,"pinned":false,"pinUrl":null},{"id":"art-0165-durant-ishbia-exchange-2026-10-17","slug":"durant-ishbia-suns-tenure-exchange-2026","headline":"Durant and Suns Owner Ishbia Spar on Social Media Over Phoenix Tenure","socialHeadline":"Kevin Durant fired back at Mat Ishbia's claim that Phoenix 'threw money at the problem' — and the Suns owner replied he never blamed him","summary":"Kevin Durant and Suns owner Mat Ishbia traded public shots on social media Saturday over how Durant's time in Phoenix is remembered. Durant, now in Houston, objected to Ishbia's CNBC Sport comments that the Suns 'threw money at the problem' acquiring him; Ishbia replied that he never blamed Durant and wished him luck.","body":["The breakup between Kevin Durant and the Phoenix Suns is still producing headlines. On Saturday, Durant — now with the Houston Rockets — publicly pushed back on comments from Suns owner Mat Ishbia about how the franchise's all-in bet on him should be remembered.","Ishbia, speaking on CNBC Sport, said Phoenix had 'thrown money at the problem' in acquiring Durant and argued that superstar talent 'doesn't necessarily lead to championships.' Durant responded on X with the hashtag '#blameKD' and a quip that Ishbia 'acts like I got arrested for a DUI or sumn,' making clear he felt singled out by the framing.","## Ishbia's reply","The Suns owner answered on the same platform, saying he did not blame Durant and wishing him luck in Houston. ESPN first reported the exchange, and TSN's AP-carried account independently confirmed the sequence of posts and their wording.","## Genuine exchange, not rumor","Both outlets treated the posts as on-record statements from the principals — a real public exchange rather than secondhand rumor. It is the latest public friction point in how Phoenix's costly, title-less Durant era is being narrated by the people who built it."],"category":"Sports","tags":["Kevin Durant","Mat Ishbia","Phoenix Suns","Houston Rockets","NBA"],"author":"NewsVeria Newsroom","publishedAt":"2026-10-18T01:49:07Z","image":{"src":"/images/media-generation-nv-durant-ishbia-2026-10-17.webp","alt":"Editorial illustration of two opposing speech-bubble shapes above a stylized basketball court circle in navy and light blue","caption":"Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.","credit":"NewsVeria generated illustration"},"sources":[{"name":"ESPN — Durant fires back at Ishbia's framing of Suns tenure","url":"https://www.espn.ph/nba/story/_/id/50203498/kd-fires-back-mat-isbhia-framing-suns-tenure","role":"primary"},{"name":"TSN (AP) — independent confirmation of the social-media exchange","url":"https://www.TSN.ca/nba/article/rockets-durant-suns-owner-ishbia-spar-on-social-media-over-durants-time-in-phoenix/","role":"corroboration"}],"status":"PUBLISHED","verification":{"eventConfirmed":true,"primarySourceFound":true,"independentCorroboration":true,"chronologyChecked":true,"detailsChecked":true,"supersededChecked":true,"imageChecked":true,"reviewer":"NewsVeria editorial desk (AI-assisted, standards in Editorial Policy)","notes":"ESPN report opened and read: Oct 17, 2026 on-record X exchange — Durant '#blameKD' post objecting to Ishbia's CNBC Sport comments ('threw money at the problem', superstar talent 'doesn't necessarily lead to championships'); Durant quip about DUI; Ishbia reply: no blame, wished luck in Houston. TSN/AP independently consistent. Dedup clean for Durant/Ishbia slugs."},"editorPick":false,"breaking":false,"pinned":false,"pinUrl":null},{"id":"art-0166-auburn-georgia-upset-2026-10-17","slug":"auburn-stuns-no1-georgia-27-24-2026","headline":"Unranked Auburn Stuns No. 1 Georgia 27-24 on Walk-Off Field Goal","socialHeadline":"Auburn 27, No. 1 Georgia 24 — a Stockton fumble, a roughing penalty and a 34-yard walk-off field goal ended Georgia's nine-game win streak over Auburn","summary":"Unranked Auburn beat No. 1 Georgia 27-24 at Sanford Stadium on Saturday on Alex McPherson's 34-yard field goal as time expired. Georgia quarterback Gunner Stockton fumbled with 58 seconds left, a roughing-the-passer penalty moved Auburn into range, and the kick ended Georgia's nine-game win streak over Auburn and 53 straight wins over unranked teams.","body":["College football's top-ranked team fell on Saturday in one of the season's biggest upsets. Unranked Auburn defeated No. 1 Georgia 27-24 at Sanford Stadium, winning on Alex McPherson's 34-yard field goal as time expired.","Georgia quarterback Gunner Stockton fumbled on a run from the Auburn 46-yard line with 58 seconds remaining, and Sylvester Smith-Reed recovered for the Tigers. On the ensuing drive, a roughing-the-passer penalty against Gabe Harris Jr. pushed Auburn into field-goal range, setting up McPherson's walk-off kick. CNN and NBC Sports both reported the same sequence and final score.","## Streaks snapped","The loss ended Georgia's nine-game winning streak over Auburn and a remarkable run of 53 consecutive wins against unranked opponents. For first-year Auburn coach Alex Golesh, it was a signature victory in his debut season on the Plains.","## A season reshaped","The result scrambles the SEC race and the national playoff picture with weeks still to play. Georgia, which entered the weekend as the sport's standard-bearer, now has to regroup on the road; Auburn has a program-defining win to build on."],"category":"Sports","tags":["Auburn","Georgia","college football","SEC","upset"],"author":"NewsVeria Newsroom","publishedAt":"2026-10-18T01:49:07Z","image":{"src":"/images/media-generation-nv-auburn-georgia-2026-10-17.webp","alt":"Editorial illustration of a football sailing through goalpost uprights with a clock-face motif in navy and light blue","caption":"Illustration: NewsVeria generated editorial illustration. NewsVeria uses clearly labelled generated illustrations and never presents imagery as real event photography.","credit":"NewsVeria generated illustration"},"sources":[{"name":"CNN — Auburn stuns No. 1 Georgia recap","url":"https://www.cnn.com/2026/10/17/sport/auburn-georgia-upset-top-ranked?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta","role":"primary"},{"name":"NBC Sports — independent recap of the late fumble and penalty","url":"https://www.nbcsports.com/college-football/news/auburn-vs-georgia-recap-results-top-ranked-bulldogs-lose-on-late-fumble-roughing-the-passer-penalty","role":"corroboration"}],"status":"PUBLISHED","verification":{"eventConfirmed":true,"primarySourceFound":true,"independentCorroboration":true,"chronologyChecked":true,"detailsChecked":true,"supersededChecked":true,"imageChecked":true,"reviewer":"NewsVeria editorial desk (AI-assisted, standards in Editorial Policy)","notes":"CNN recap opened and read: Auburn 27-24 over No.1 Georgia, Sanford Stadium, Oct 17, 2026; McPherson 34-yd FG as time expired; Stockton fumble at Auburn 46 with 0:58 (recovered by Sylvester Smith-Reed); roughing-the-passer on Gabe Harris Jr. set up kick; ended 9-game Auburn streak and 53 straight wins over unranked teams; Alex Golesh first-year coach. NBC Sports independently consistent. Dedup clean."},"editorPick":true,"breaking":true,"pinned":false,"pinUrl":null}]